CAT = "cadcEthics"; SEG = "ETH"
Q = [
("A client invites his counselor to become a paid partner in his business. Accepting would create a:",
 "Dual relationship", ["Therapeutic alliance", "Collateral contact", "Peer referral"],
 "A business tie with a client adds a second relationship that can cloud judgment and exploit the client. Counselors decline such offers."),
("A counselor in recovery tells her whole relapse story at length in a first session. The main concern is that:",
 "Focus shifts to the counselor", ["It is too brief to help", "It breaks a federal law", "It shows too little empathy"],
 "Self-disclosure that is long or early puts the counselor's needs at the center. Disclosure should be rare, brief and aimed at the client's goals."),
("At discharge, a client offers his counselor an expensive watch. The counselor should:",
 "Decline and explain why", ["Accept it quietly", "Sell it for the agency", "Accept it and mention it later"],
 "Valuable gifts can create obligation and blur boundaries. Declining warmly and explaining the policy protects the relationship."),
("A current client sends the counselor a friend request on social media. The counselor should:",
 "Decline and discuss it", ["Accept to build rapport", "Accept but never post", "Block without a word"],
 "Online friendships with clients create a dual relationship and privacy risks. Declining and talking about it in session keeps the boundary clear."),
("A client asks her counselor whether she should lower her antidepressant dose. The counselor should:",
 "Refer her to the prescriber", ["Suggest a lower dose", "Tell her to stop it", "Look it up online and advise her"],
 "Medication decisions are outside an addiction counselor's scope. The counselor supports the client in talking with her prescriber."),
("A client discloses an eating disorder the counselor is not trained to treat. The counselor should:",
 "Consult and refer as needed", ["Treat it using a self-help book", "Ignore the disclosure", "End all counseling"],
 "Counselors work within their training. Consulting a supervisor and arranging a referral, while continuing SUD care, serves the client."),
("A counselor is assigned a client who is her neighbor's son. The counselor should:",
 "Raise it in supervision", ["Hide the connection", "See him at his home", "Tell the neighbor"],
 "A prior personal connection is a possible conflict of interest. Raising it with a supervisor lets the agency decide whether to reassign the case."),
("A counselor refers clients to a sober living home she secretly owns. This is a:",
 "Conflict of interest", ["Case management duty", "Standard referral", "Recovery support plan"],
 "Profiting from referrals without disclosure puts the counselor's interests ahead of the client's. Referrals should be based only on client need."),
("The main purpose of clinical supervision is to:",
 "Protect clients, build skills", ["Monitor billing only", "Replace all training", "Set staff pay and schedules"],
 "Supervision safeguards client welfare and supports the counselor's professional growth, including ethical decision making."),
("A counselor is unsure whether a situation calls for a report. Her best next step is to:",
 "Consult her supervisor", ["Decide alone quickly", "Let the client decide", "Ask an online forum for advice"],
 "Consultation helps with hard ethical and legal calls while protecting the client's privacy, and the consultation should be documented."),
("A counselor realizes she assumes all clients share her religious views. She most needs to build:",
 "Cultural self-awareness", ["Stronger confrontation", "A shorter intake", "Firmer group rules"],
 "Multicultural competence starts with awareness of one's own beliefs and biases so they are not imposed on clients."),
("A client's culture expects her family to share in major decisions. With her consent, the counselor should:",
 "Include the family she chooses", ["Exclude all relatives", "Let the family decide alone", "Refer her to a cultural program"],
 "A multicultural approach adapts care to the client's values. The client chooses who is involved, and consent covers what is shared."),
("A client with limited English needs counseling services. The best practice is to use:",
 "A trained interpreter", ["The client's child", "A bilingual friend from group", "Simple English only"],
 "Trained interpreters give accurate, confidential communication. Children and peers can distort meaning and should not carry sensitive information."),
("A counselor notices clients from one ethnic group leave treatment early more often. The best response is to:",
 "Review barriers and adapt", ["Add confrontation groups", "Refer them all elsewhere", "Require longer intakes"],
 "Advocating for equity in care means looking for program barriers, such as language, cost or a poor cultural fit, and changing them."),
("Why should counselors avoid calling drug test results clean or dirty?",
 "The terms add to stigma", ["The terms are illegal", "The terms confuse labs", "The terms are too informal"],
 "Calling results positive or negative is accurate and avoids implying that a person is dirty, which feeds shame and stigma."),
("A counselor in recovery begins drinking again. The ethical step is to:",
 "Seek help and limit practice", ["Keep working and hide it", "Tell clients in group", "Resign without notice that day"],
 "Counselors have a duty not to practice while impaired. Getting help and stepping back from clients protects them while she recovers."),
("A counselor sees a current client at a 12-step meeting they both attend. This is best handled by:",
 "Discussing it with the client", ["Pretending not to know him", "Talking about his sessions", "Asking him to leave"],
 "Overlapping roles are common in recovery communities. Talking openly with the client about how to handle meeting each other protects confidentiality and trust."),
("Which action is an example of advocating for a client?",
 "Removing access barriers", ["Deciding for the client", "Testifying without consent", "Raising program fees"],
 "Advocacy means acting with and for clients to reduce barriers to care, such as working with a program to accept a client on medication."),
("A counselor keeps her skills current mainly through:",
 "Training and supervision", ["Years of practice alone", "Her own recovery alone", "Reading old case files"],
 "Competence is maintained through continuing education and supervision. Lived experience can help but does not replace professional training."),
("A client offers to repair the counselor's car instead of paying for sessions. The main concern is that:",
 "It blurs professional roles", ["Cars are hard to repair well", "It costs the agency money", "It speeds up treatment"],
 "Bartering services creates a second relationship and can lead to conflict if the work goes badly, so most ethics guidance discourages it."),
("Sexual contact between a counselor and a current client is:",
 "Never acceptable", ["Fine with consent", "Fine after one month", "Up to the client"],
 "Sexual relationships with current clients are prohibited in every counseling code, whatever the client says, because of the power difference."),
("Which task is outside a typical addiction counselor's scope of practice?",
 "Prescribing medication", ["Leading a group", "Writing a treatment plan", "Making referrals"],
 "Counselors assess, plan, counsel and refer. Prescribing is for licensed prescribers, though counselors support clients taking medication."),
("Culturally responsive treatment means:",
 "Fitting care to values", ["Lowering care standards", "Changing the diagnosis", "Avoiding family topics"],
 "Culturally responsive care adapts how services are delivered to a client's culture and values while keeping the same standards of quality."),
("A counselor strongly dislikes a client's political views. The best way to keep this from affecting care is to:",
 "Notice it and set it aside", ["Debate the client", "Hint at her own views", "Shorten his sessions"],
 "Self-awareness lets a counselor recognize her reactions and keep them from shaping treatment. If she cannot, she seeks supervision."),
]
